<http://www.example.org/muac/ontology/local-moo> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Ontology> .
<http://www.example.org/muac/ontology/local-moo> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Convention for naming relations (Object Properties)\n\n1. The Active-Passive Pattern (most relations)\n* Forward Relation: Active verb in the present tense (e.g., employs, teaches, owns).\n  - Points from the independent entity (Whole), active agent (Actor), or creator to the   dependent entity (Part), passive object (Target), or creation. \n* Reverse Relation: Passive voice or reciprocal verb (e.g., is_employed_by, is_taught_by, is_owned_by).\nExample:\n - Forward: Organization employs Person\n - Reverse: Person is_employed_by Organization\n\n2. The Has-Is-Of Pattern (hiearchical relations)\n* Forward Relation: Starts with has_ to indicate ownership, composition, or property.\n* Reverse Relation: Starts with is_ or ends with _of to point back to the subject.\nExample:\n - Forward: Car has_part Engine\n - Reverse: Engine is_part_of Car\n\nDetermines property direction based on priority: Forward relations point from the independent entity (Whole), active agent (Actor), or creator to the dependent entity (Part), passive object (Target), or creation." .
<http://www.example.org/muac/ontology/local-moo> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "TODO:\n- Capabilities are realized by Flows\n- Function Exchanges are allocated to Structure Exchanges" .
